From ee7de40a610b4d177dcf7a5efd83b8e4f28394c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Qingfang Deng <dqfext@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:34:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] tls: Pipeline TLS 1.3 software receive decryption

TLS 1.3 currently waits for each receive decryption because its content
type and padding are encrypted. Speculating across a KeyUpdate also
requires retaining ciphertext and deferring authentication errors until
the record can be interpreted in wire order.

Collect up to eight available records and submit independent buffered
AEAD requests with snapshots of their record sequence numbers. Drain
requests under the socket lock, then process their results in wire
order. Keep later ciphertext across a KeyUpdate, discard speculative
plaintext, and retry after userspace installs the new receive key.
Only an error at the head of the queue becomes fatal.

Integrate the pending queue with receive, splice, read_sock, readiness,
rekey and cleanup paths. When the consecutive-empty-record limit aborts
the stream, discard pending records and the parser's lookahead so that
prefetched data cannot bypass the limit. Preserve the TLS 1.2 path and
synchronous device fallback. Document that asynchronous TLS 1.3 uses
kernel buffers even with TLS_RX_EXPECT_NO_PAD enabled. Eight records is
an initial bounded batch size, not a benchmark-selected value.

Extend the existing TLS selftest with queued key generations, corrupted
data after rekey, padded records containing empty application data, and
a longer empty-record burst exercising the parser's lookahead.

Original validation on QEMU TCG with native and pcrypt-wrapped AEADs:
- The complete suite passed 977/977 tests with each backend, using the
  standard 30-second selftest timeout and a kernel without KASAN.
- All receive tests passed in the 176-case pcrypt run under KASAN and
  lockdep. The only failure was the transmit-only recseq_wrap test,
  independently reproduced on the kernel before the receive fix
  (9 failures in 30 runs). No kernel diagnostics or timeouts occurred.
- The preceding receive fix independently passed all 26 tls_err tests
  under KASAN and lockdep before this commit was applied.
- Earlier tracing observed seven outstanding decryptions and successful
  speculative rekey retries.
- Kernel/selftest builds, git diff --check and checkpatch.pl passed.

OpenAI Codex assisted with the implementation, tests, documentation and
this changelog, following requests to implement TLS 1.3 RX pipelining,
validate it with existing selftests under QEMU TCG using pcrypt, and
rebase onto master, and place pipelining after the standalone async
receive error-accounting fix.

[6.18: Apply after the standalone async receive fix and the parser and
empty-record prerequisites. Preserve psock readiness and synchronous
BPF receive handling, draining any batch queued before BPF attachment.
Move decryption error aborts into tls_rx_one_record() as on mainline.
Include bad_auth_splice coverage, and omit unrelated TX payload-limit
documentation. OpenAI Codex assisted with these backport adjustments.]

Backport validation on QEMU TCG:
- Complete existing selftests: 930/930 with native crypto and 930/930 with
  pcrypt, using a 120-second harness timeout without KASAN or lockdep.
  No test timeouts or kernel diagnostics occurred in either full run.
- The focused KASAN run passed 170/170 on rerun. An initial run failed
  two 128_128 empty-record cases; targeted reruns and twenty repeated
  cases passed. The initial failures were not reproduced or explained.
- Debug pcrypt runs report a known padata lockdep false positive, also
  reproduced with the unmodified 6.18.63 kernel. Lockdep disables itself
  after that warning, limiting its coverage; KASAN remains active.
- Kernel/selftest builds, git diff --check and checkpatch.pl passed.

